  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'A ''pol'' is a traditional gated residential cluster where families

    of
    the same community, caste, or profession live together

    behind a shared entrance.
    More than a neighborhood, it

    functions as a single, extended household.


    In
    Navsari Parsi ''pols'', houses share common walls and

    feature deep, elevated
    front verandas known as ''otlas''. The

    lanes fill with the fragrance of
    loban (frankincense) and

    sandalwood. Parsi women sweep their thresholds
    and adorn

    them with ''chalki'' intricate powder-dusted designs of fish,

    flowers,
    and stars to welcome good spirits.


    Hidden within the timber-framed homes
    are private wells

    that supply the pure water essential for the community''s

    daily
    rituals.


    Navsari has five priestly Parsi ''pols'', home to 145 families.

    These
    are Aashaa, Maahyaar, Chaandaa, Pahlan Anna (also

    known as Kaka Pahlan),
    and Kaka Dhanpal.


    This Polea Desai door was a part of the Polea Desai pol

    Gate,
    the ceremonial entrance to Desaivad, the historic

    quarter associated with
    Navsari''s influential Desai family.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
